<p>
Irreal, in both its incarnations, has always used a dynamic Web site: first on <a href="https://www.blogger.com/about/">Blogger</a> and now on <a href="https://wordpress.org/download/">WordPress</a>. I like them both. They&rsquo;re easy to use and, really, perfect for non-technical people who want to blog. At this point, Irreal will probably stay on WordPress throughout its lifetime.
</p>
<p>
Still, I occasionally think that it would be nice to change to a static web site. The problem with dynamic Websites is that they&rsquo;re a black box driven by a database and it&rsquo;s hard to understand how things work, how to customize them, and how to do fundamental things like backing up your site.
</p>
<p>
Of course, static sites come with their own problems and difficulties. Recently, Bastien Guerry, one of the Org mode heroes, introduced his own static site generator, <a href="https://codeberg.org/bzg/orgy">Orgy</a>. He has a nice post <a href="https://bzg.fr/en/notes/orgy-tutorial/">that steps you through setting up an Orgy site from scratch</a>. Orgy seems extremely easy to use. You write your blog posts in Org mode, call Orgy, and everything but moving it to your hosting provider is taken care of. You get an index, RSS, tag support, search and more. Take a look at Guerry&rsquo;s post for the details.
</p>
<p>
The thing I really like about it is that there&rsquo;s no database. All your post sources stay safely on your own machine and you can back them up with whatever method(s) you prefer. Even if you have to regenerate your entire site, it&rsquo;s only an Orgy call away. There&rsquo;s no PHP to wade through to. The output of Orgy is simply your HTML and supporting files. It&rsquo;s simplicity itself. If you don&rsquo;t need a bunch of fancy plugins, Orgy may be just what you&rsquo;re looking for.</p>
